WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.180
The first film followed an allele because it sat on the X. Can parent of origin

2
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.120
matter beyond the sex chromosomes?

3
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.220
It can, in two different ways.

4
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.840
The key is to ask whether transmission or expression changed.

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.400
In the first lane, organelle inheritance changes which DNA arrives.

6
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.580
In the second, genomic imprinting changes which parental nuclear allele is

7
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.740
expressed. Keep transmission and expression separate.

8
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.820
Both mechanisms make pedigrees depend on parent, but for different physical reasons.

9
00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:46.743
Mitochondria and chloroplasts carry small genomes outside the nucleus.

10
00:00:47.963 --> 00:00:51.243
An egg supplies abundant cytoplasm and organelles.

11
00:00:52.423 --> 00:00:55.723
A male gamete usually contributes far less cytoplasm.

12
00:00:56.983 --> 00:01:01.523
That difference gives maternal organelles an enormous numerical head start.

13
00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:08.103
Many species also exclude, deplete, or destroy paternal organelles or their DNA.

14
00:01:09.763 --> 00:01:13.283
Organelle genomes are therefore generally inherited from the mother.

15
00:01:14.523 --> 00:01:17.483
The mother's organelle pattern reaches sons and daughters.

16
00:01:18.763 --> 00:01:23.263
Some species instead show paternal or biparental organelle transmission.

17
00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:27.873
Both sexes can inherit a maternal organelle variant.

18
00:01:29.193 --> 00:01:31.793
Each child now carries the mother's organelle DNA.

19
00:01:33.113 --> 00:01:36.173
But carrying a variant is not the same as passing it on.

20
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:38.707
Imprinting is different.

21
00:01:39.907 --> 00:01:41.567
An imprinted gene is nuclear.

22
00:01:42.887 --> 00:01:47.107
Both parents transmit a copy, but only one parental copy is expressed.

23
00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:50.387
A parent-of-origin mark decides which.

24
00:01:51.607 --> 00:01:53.907
The mark does not change the DNA sequence.

25
00:01:55.247 --> 00:02:00.067
The marks are set during egg or sperm formation, then reset in the next generation.

27
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:05.687
Both a daughter and a son inherit the same maternal organelle variant.

28
00:02:06.987 --> 00:02:12.607
Each carries it. Which child can place that variant into the next generation's eggs?

29
00:02:14.047 --> 00:02:16.487
Trace the lineage, not the child's own phenotype.

30
00:02:17.787 --> 00:02:20.467
The question is about transmission, not expression.

31
00:02:26.583 --> 00:02:30.643
The daughter can usually pass it through eggs into another generation.

32
00:02:31.983 --> 00:02:36.403
The son can carry the variant, but his sperm usually do not transmit those

33
00:02:36.403 --> 00:02:42.383
organelles. The children can therefore share a variant while only one sex continues

34
00:02:42.383 --> 00:02:43.383
its lineage.

35
00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:45.653
Now compare the lanes.

36
00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:50.833
Organelle inheritance usually filters transmission through the egg.

37
00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:57.133
Imprinting transmits both nuclear copies, then filters expression by parental

38
00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:01.633
origin. These mechanisms can produce different pedigrees.

39
00:03:02.717 --> 00:03:08.277
Inheritance patterns depend on allele identity, chromosome location, and parental

40
00:03:08.277 --> 00:03:14.717
origin. Here, parent of origin can decide which DNA arrives or which allele acts.

41
00:03:16.037 --> 00:03:20.917
Most nuclear genes use both parental copies, so imprinting is a special case.

42
00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:25.107
When a pedigree depends on the mother, ask what is moving.

43
00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:28.547
Is organelle DNA traveling through eggs?

44
00:03:29.747 --> 00:03:34.487
Or are both nuclear alleles present while a parental mark controls expression?
